# Tool input validation

| Field | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Issue | re-cinq/HALEngine#37 |
| Status | Draft |

The registry enforces the same JSON Schema that it supplies to model providers, allowing malformed calls to be corrected through ordinary tool results.

## Contract

1. "'`ToolRegistry.execute` rejects missing required properties, incorrect declared types and invalid enum members before invoking an executor, returning a `ToolResponse` whose result names the property path and constraint without exposing input values; rejection logs likewise name paths and constraints without values, valid input including unknown properties reaches the executor unchanged exactly once without defaults or coercion, and an unknown tool still throws `Unknown tool: <name>`.
2. Static schemas compile at registration, with an uncompilable schema throwing an error naming its tool at registration, and successful compiled schemas are reused across ten executions without rereading the schema.
3. Function-valued definitions resolve for each execution and validate against the current schema object, including when switching back to a previously resolved object.

## Delivery obligations

The execute method points here with a one-line doc comment; README.md, docs/adding-a-tool.md and .specify/spec.md describe validation, extra-property pass-through, rejection as a tool result and the existing five-round retry bound. The validator ships as a runtime dependency and the built package remains importable. Release documentation carries the executor migration note, a consumer-facing changelog and a minor version bump. A tool-loop regression demonstrates a rejected call followed by a corrected retry resolving processMessage with rejection text supplied to the next provider round.
EOF
